# X710 CSD / IPI wedge diagnostic fragment — NOT part of the default build.
#
# Round 34 of the CPU-wedge investigation. Selected with
# `GTS9_DIAG_FRAGMENT=kernel/config/gts9wifi-csd-lock.fragment`, which merges
# this on top of the stock seed and the mainline fragment. The default build
# does not read this file at all, so a production kernel cannot acquire
# CONFIG_CSD_LOCK_WAIT_DEBUG by accident.
#
# WHAT THIS ADDS, AND WHY IT IS THE RIGHT INSTRUMENT
#
# docs/CSD_IPI_WEDGE_PLAN.md has the full argument. In one paragraph: the wedge
# is a CPU that stops answering an ordinary IPI, and the reported stack
# (toggle_allocation_gate -> kick_all_cpus_sync -> smp_call_function_many_cond)
# is the *canary* - a CPU waiting inside smp_call_function_many_cond() for
# another CPU that never completes. CONFIG_CSD_LOCK_WAIT_DEBUG instruments
# exactly that wait:
#
#   kernel/smp.c: smp_call_function_many_cond() calls csd_lock_wait(csd) for
#   every target CPU when SCF_WAIT is set, and with this option csd_lock_wait()
#   becomes __csd_lock_wait() -> csd_lock_wait_toolong(), which after
#   csd_lock_timeout milliseconds reports:
#
#     which CPU is waiting (raw_smp_processor_id)
#     which CPU it is waiting for (csd_lock_wait_getcpu -> csd->node.dst)
#     the CSD function and its argument (csd->func, csd->info)
#     whether the target is handling THIS request, a PRIOR one, or nothing
#     a stack of the sender, and dump_cpu_task() of the target
#
# That is a strictly earlier and more specific signal than anything the project
# has captured. RCU notices at onset+21 s; the first CSD report fires at
# csd_lock_timeout, which is 5 s by default - i.e. BEFORE the RCU stall, while
# the target CPU is still wedged and its state is still readable.
#
# THE PARAMETERS, READ OUT OF THE PINNED TREE RATHER THAN ASSUMED
#
#   csdlock_debug=       __setup, boolean-ish (kernel/smp.c:168). Enables the
#                        static branch. `=1` turns it on. CSD_LOCK_WAIT_DEBUG
#                        alone does NOT enable it at runtime - the static key
#                        defaults to CONFIG_CSD_LOCK_WAIT_DEBUG_DEFAULT, which
#                        is itself default n. Both are set below, so the
#                        diagnostic is live without depending on a cmdline
#                        token; the token is still passed as a belt-and-braces
#                        identity marker and its presence is checked on device.
#   csd_lock_timeout=    module_param, ulong, MILLISECONDS, default 5000
#                        (kernel/smp.c:174). Deliberately left at the default:
#                        a shorter value reports sooner but also re-reports more
#                        often, and the first report is what carries the state.
#   panic_on_ipistall=   module_param, int, MILLISECONDS, default 0 = disabled
#                        (kernel/smp.c:176). DELIBERATELY LEFT AT 0. The brief is
#                        explicit that round one collects information rather
#                        than making the instrument panic earlier, and the
#                        existing softlockup_panic=1 -> panic=10 chain already
#                        guarantees automatic recovery.
#
# Note for whoever wires the cmdline: csd_lock_timeout and panic_on_ipistall are
# module_param, so they are reachable as `csd_lock_timeout=` /
# `panic_on_ipistall=` only because smp.c is built in - and, unlike __setup
# parameters, they are also writable at runtime under
# /sys/module/smp/parameters/. csdlock_debug= is an __setup parameter and is
# NOT writable after boot.

# The instrument itself. depends on DEBUG_KERNEL (y) and SMP (y) and 64BIT (y),
# and the 64BIT dependency is load-bearing rather than incidental: csd->node.dst
# exists only `#ifdef CONFIG_64BIT` (include/linux/smp_types.h), and
# csd_lock_wait_getcpu() returns it as the target CPU. Without it every report
# would name cpu = -1 and the whole exercise would be pointless.
CONFIG_CSD_LOCK_WAIT_DEBUG=y
# Turn it on at boot without needing a cmdline token that ABL could drop. This
# is the switch that makes the static branch default-enabled; it exists for
# exactly this purpose and is `default n` so nothing else acquires it.
CONFIG_CSD_LOCK_WAIT_DEBUG_DEFAULT=y

# --- what is deliberately NOT added -----------------------------------------
#
# No ftrace/tracer options. Round one is CSD only, so that a result is
# attributable to one instrument; the targeted-trace profile is round two's
# business (docs/CSD_IPI_WEDGE_PLAN.md section 5) and gets its own fragment.
#
# No CONFIG_IRQSOFF_TRACER / PREEMPT_TRACER. They are the third layer in the
# brief's priority order and are explicitly not to be enabled alongside CSD
# debug on a first run.
#
# No CONFIG_FUNCTION_TRACER. It is n today, and turning it on would add
# mcount call sites across the whole kernel - a large observer effect for an
# instrument this round does not need.
#
# No printk/loglevel change, no debug in any driver, and no change to
# CONFIG_PANIC_TIMEOUT, the softlockup detector or the workqueue stall panic.
# The recovery chain is the one already verified on hardware:
# softlockup_panic=1 -> panic=10 -> automatic reboot.
